    "homePage": {
      "title1": "Acinetobacter baumannii",
      "content1": "Acinetobacter baumannii is a Gram-negative, aerobic bacterium belonging to the Neisseriaceae family. This bacterium can live on the body of a healthy person, residing in the throat, skin, or bodily secretions without causing disease.",
      "content2": "However, under favorable conditions such as when the body's immune system is weakened, Acinetobacter baumannii can become a disease-causing agent.\n\nAcinetobacter baumannii is capable of surviving for a long time in the external environment and is resistant to several disinfectants commonly used in hospitals, making standard cleaning agents ineffective against it. This makes it one of the most difficult pathogens to treat effectively.\n\nIn particular, this bacterium can resist almost all antibiotics used to treat common Acinetobacter infections, leading to major challenges in treatment and a rising mortality rate due to hospital-acquired infections.",
      "title2": "Who is at risk of infection?",
      "content3": "Anyone can become infected with Acinetobacter baumannii. You are more likely to get an Acinetobacter baumannii infection if you have underlying conditions such as diabetes or COPD. Additionally, your risk increases if you:\n\n+ Have a weakened immune system.\n\n+ Practice poor hygiene, such as improper or infrequent hand washing.\n\n+ Have stayed in an intensive care unit or are using a ventilator.\n\n+ Have open wounds from injuries or accidents.\n\n+ Are in close contact with someone infected with Acinetobacter baumannii.\n\n+ Have used antibiotics and been hospitalized for a long period.\n\n+ Undergo treatment involving invasive devices such as urinary catheters.",
      "title3": "What illnesses does Acinetobacter baumannii cause?",
      "content4": "Acinetobacter baumannii can cause serious and sometimes life-threatening infections. You may not realize you are infected until you experience the following issues:\n\n+ Pneumonia\n\n+ Bloodstream infection\n\n+ Meningitis\n\n+ Urinary tract infection\n\n+ Skin or wound infection",
      "title4": "Signs and symptoms",
      "content5": "Infection with Acinetobacter baumannii can present the following signs and symptoms:\n\n+ Fever\n\n+ Redness, swelling, heat, or pain at a skin area or wound\n\n+ Rough, orange-colored skin with blisters\n\n+ Cough, chest pain, or difficulty breathing\n\n+ Burning sensation when urinating\n\n+ Drowsiness, headache, or stiff neck",
      "title5": "Preventing the spread of infection",
      "content6": "Handwashing: Use soap and water to wash your hands after using the restroom, before eating, and after coughing or sneezing. Use antibacterial hand sanitizer if water is unavailable. Wash your hands regularly.\n\nKeep wounds covered: Ensure all wounds are clean and covered with a bandage until they are fully healed.\n\nCover open wounds properly: Disinfect wounds thoroughly and cover them with a clean bandage. Change the bandage daily until healing occurs.\n\nAlways follow your doctor’s instructions when using antibiotics: Take the full course at the prescribed dosage, even if you start to feel better. Bacteria can become resistant (harder to kill) if you don’t follow medical instructions. Never use antibiotics without a doctor’s prescription."
    },
